* Find an easy method to connect the command (and the input) to the output.

use cases:
- Show the output of a given command and input.
- Find a string in the outputs of a given command. For example: which failed outputs of command X
  contain the string Y?

problems:
- A file name cannot hold the command and the input, because some characters are not permitted.
- The output file is not a good location for the command, because a search of the output can find
  the command by accident.

options:
- Make a symbolic link, beside the hash, with a shorter name.
- Write the command and the input in the first line of the file.
- Make an SQLite database, or a simple file, that connects the command and the input to the hash.

current idea:
- Make a subdirectory with the name of the job hash. Put one file in this subdirectory, with a safe
  version of the command and the input. The MTIME of this file gives the time of the last run.

* Use SQLite to hold most of the btree when dispatch defers the reruns. This prevents unlimited use of
memory.
Bigset is not applicable, because its structure cannot sort the data. Make a similar component that can
add an item and remove the smallest item.
The component can keep a small number of items in memory, but this is possibly not necessary. The time to
find the next job is very short in comparison to the duration of a job. Also, the channel usually holds one
job. Therefore there is a delay only when another program makes many new jobs quickly.

* Make zstd optional.
The marker functions must then ignore the suffix, and find the marker with or without `.zstd`.
This makes it more difficult to search these files with grep or other tools. Therefore it is possibly
better to always compress the output, and keep the behaviour the same for each file.
Note: `dispatch.Decompress` is now available. It reads the output that `Compress` wrote.

* Only unit tests cover the escalation of the signals (SIGTERM, SIGKILL, process group). These tests send
the signals directly to a Worker. A test that starts the real program, and then sends more than one SIGINT,
gives better evidence. But such a test is difficult to make reliable in CI.
